                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A nostalgic throwback to Game Boy era platformers, pairing chiptune music, simple two-button controls, and one-hit deaths with tough but short stages\n\nThis game feels like it was locked away for ages, with origins that trace back to the Game Boy era. When you start the game, you are welcomed by a small box in the middle of the screen, which can fortunately be expanded to full screen without losing any detail. There is also chiptune-like music and gameplay that revolves around just two keys.\n\nAside from moving left and right, your only actions are jumping, including a double jump, and throwing shurikens. There are no restrictions or mechanics borrowed from modern games, such as stamina systems, so you can throw as many shurikens as you like. Despite its simple feature set and minimalist design, the game places a strong emphasis on difficulty, something many players who grew up with these kinds of titles will recognize immediately.\n\nThe main challenge is that you can only take one hit. It does not matter whether the source is an enemy or an environmental hazard; the moment you touch anything, you dissolve into a cloud of smoke. This is closely tied to the game’s so-called limitation, which can hardly be called one, as this is simply how games were designed at the time.\n\nYour view is restricted to only a few steps ahead, meaning enemies often appear the instant you jump over an obstacle. Without fast reflexes or prior knowledge, you will likely die several times until you memorize the layout of each stage. Your shurikens only reach the edge of the visible screen, so anything beyond that cannot be defeated, even if you know it is there.\n\nOn the positive side, stages are short and never overstay their welcome. You will encounter moving and disappearing platforms that can drop you into the void or onto hazards, but there are no excessively complex jump sequences. The game also includes checkpoints, though they are unmarked, leaving you unsure of when progress has been saved.\n\nFor those who enjoy collectibles, each stage hides a single coin that eventually unlocks secret levels. Since the game is linear, these can be easily missed. There are five themed worlds, and while they introduce fun variations such as snorkeling in underwater stages or flying through sky levels, the entire game can be completed fairly quickly. You also do not need to worry about beating the game with only a few limited lives, as death simply respawns you at the last checkpoint.",
